# THE GUARD REVISION THIS REPOSITORY AUDITED, BY CONTENT.
#
# .github/workflows/release.yml pins the shared release guards by commit
# SHA. A SHA is content-addressed and therefore immutable, so the pin
# cannot be edited under us. What a SHA does NOT do is tell a reader what
# is in it, and nothing states which BYTES were looked at before pinning
# them. That is what this file is for.
#
# Every file the guards execute is recorded below by SHA-256, taken at
# the pinned revision. tests/test_guard_pins.sh re-fetches each one and
# refuses any difference, so:
#
#   * bumping the pin without re-auditing goes RED, because the digests
#     below describe a revision the workflow no longer names;
#   * a pin that resolves to bytes other than the audited ones goes RED,
#     which is the falsifiable form of "we ran what we pinned";
#   * the check runs offline of any claim the guards make about
#     themselves, before a tag is ever pushed.
#
# WHAT REMAINS TRUSTED, stated plainly rather than papered over: that
# GitHub runs the workflow file living at the pinned SHA. A workflow
# cannot establish its own identity from inside itself, because a
# substituted file could simply print the expected answer. That is the
# platform's guarantee and the reason `uses:@<sha>` is written by SHA.
# Everything downstream of it is checked here by content.
#
# THE DIGESTS BELOW WERE WRITTEN BY `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh`, which
# fetched each file at 2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55 and hashed it. Do not
# hand-edit them, and do not copy this file from another repository: a
# copied record passes every check while having audited nothing, because
# the digests are genuinely the pinned revision's bytes. What a copy
# cannot carry is the reading.
#
# TO RE-AUDIT AFTER A PIN BUMP: read the diff, then re-run
# `bash fleet/guard_pins.sh <this repository>` and rewrite the note below.
#
#   https://github.com/nelsonduarte/capa-language/compare/<old>...2db00705e05b1774cf41eb58006e6533cfca4d55

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# AUDIT NOTE.
#
# THIS IS A FIRST PIN, NOT A BUMP. This repository has never called the
# shared guards, so there is no previous revision and no diff to read.
# 2db0070 is "fix(ci): job.workflow_sha, and stop the guards claiming a
# proof they cannot have (#91)", and it is the same revision capa_url
# and capa_uuid pin. Confirmed to be an ancestor of the compiler's main
# at 5e087e1 (`git merge-base --is-ancestor`).
#
# ALL FIVE FILES BELOW WERE READ IN FULL at this revision, 1171 lines,
# and their digests were also recomputed locally from git objects and
# matched what fleet/guard_pins.sh fetched. What the reading found, in
# the order it matters for THIS package:
#
#   tools/capa_floor.sh (80 lines). Reads exactly one `capa` key from
#   the [package] table, accepts only "X.Y.Z" or ">=X.Y.Z", and fails
#   closed on anything else. This is what chooses the compiler the clean
#   room downloads, so it is what turns capa.toml's `>=1.18.1` into a
#   real download. RUN AGAINST THIS REPOSITORY'S NEW MANIFEST, at this
#   revision: prints `1.18.1`, exit 0. Two things about it are STALE
#   here and neither is load-bearing. Its header says "nothing in the
#   compiler ENFORCES this field today"; 1.19.0 does, and the script
#   only reads and prints, so its behaviour is unchanged. Its version
#   regex `[0-9][0-9.]*[0-9]` also accepts 1.17 and 1.2.3.4, tightened
#   later in the compiler's c5f6764; `>=1.18.1` parses identically under
#   both, so the loose form cannot mis-select a compiler for us.
#
#   tools/clean_room_build.sh (223 lines). Extracts the tarball into an
#   absolute, EMPTY room, requires exactly one top-level entry, unsets
#   CAPA_PATH, cd's in and runs each --run with the status read
#   explicitly. This is precisely the isolation this package's floor
#   depends on: with no siblings and no CAPA_PATH, `import
#   capa_test.testing` in tests/ can only resolve through PR #85's
#   project-root-by-manifest-name fallback, which is why the clean room
#   is where a pre-1.18.0 compiler fails and a development checkout is
#   not. Zero --run commands is an error, which is what stops an empty
#   flow reporting success.
#
#   tools/check_tag_version.sh (112 lines). Table-scoped awk read of
#   `version` from [package], refusing zero or more than one match, so a
#   dependency's own version line cannot be compared against the tag.
#   RUN AGAINST THIS REPOSITORY'S NEW MANIFEST, at this revision: `v0.2.0`
#   passes and `v0.1.1` is refused with the version it read. That matters
#   here because the new capa.toml carries roughly 130 lines of comment
#   between [package] and [capabilities], and this confirms none of it is
#   parsed as a second version or a second floor.
#
#   tools/verify_guard_digests.sh (148 lines). Compares the fetched
#   guard tree against the digests release.yml passes. Fails closed on a
#   malformed line, an absolute or `..`-bearing path, a missing file, a
#   mismatch, and on an EMPTY list, which is the case that would
#   otherwise render "verified nothing" as "verified". Its own header is
#   explicit that it cannot establish the workflow's identity from
#   inside the workflow.
#
#   .github/workflows/release-guards.yml (608 lines). Two jobs,
#   `permissions: {}` at the workflow level and `contents: read` on
#   each, no id-token anywhere. Every input reaches the shell through
#   `env:` rather than `${{ }}` interpolation. `job.workflow_sha` and
#   `job.workflow_repository` are checked for emptiness BEFORE the
#   checkout, because an empty ref makes actions/checkout fall back to
#   the default branch; that empty-string failure is what this very
#   revision exists to fix. The compiler is downloaded as the released
#   linux binary for the floor version and `gh attestation verify`d
#   before use. One consequence specific to capa_test: the GH_TOKEN
#   passed to guard 2 exists so `capa install` can verify a
#   dependency's SLSA provenance, and this package has no dependencies,
#   so that branch does nothing here.
#
# WHAT I DID NOT DO: dispatch guard-selftest.yml. It cannot go green
# against either existing tag, for the reasons written out in that
# file's header, and no v0.2.0 tag exists yet.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

